# Prompt caching on LithosAI

Whether the cache needs a *fixed prefix*, and what editing a turn does to it.
Measured 2026-10-05 against `api.lithosai.cloud`, `deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash`,
with `research/cache_probe.zig`: eight user turns of random filler (~19k prompt
tokens), one variant per edit, each variant sent twice.

```
zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig cache_probe
```

| request | prompt_tokens | cached_tokens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| base (cold) | 18771 | — |
| base again | 18771 | 18688 |
| last turn edited | 18777 | 15360 |
| last turn edited again | 18777 | — |
| middle turn edited | 18771 | — |
| middle turn edited again | 18771 | — |
| first turn edited | 18742 | — |
| first turn edited again | 18742 | — |

## What it says

- *The cache is prefix-based.* Editing the last turn still kept 15360 of 18777
  tokens cached — everything before the edit. The edited turn and everything
  after it are reprocessed; nothing earlier is lost.
- *A first-turn edit caches nothing*, which is the same statement from the other
  end: with no unchanged prefix, there is nothing to hit.
- *Even a full hit has a tail.* Identical bytes cached 18688 of 18771, so the
  final block is not cached.
- *A hit is opportunistic.* Three variants reported no cache at all on a repeat
  of bytes that had just been cached. Requests are routed by llm-d across
  replicas, each holding its own cache, so consecutive requests need not land on
  the one that was warmed. The cache is an optimisation to exploit, not a
  resource to plan around.

For the turn model (`research/turns.dj`) the consequence is narrow and useful:
editing a turn costs from that turn onward, not the whole conversation.

## The principle this serves

harness1 busts the prefix cache rather than let a running prompt see stale data — the
project principle in `readme.dj`. The measurement above is what makes that
affordable: an edit invalidates from the edit onward and nothing before it, so
the price of never serving a stale turn is bounded by where the turn changed.